    {
      "id": "90bf4d716e380d78d26e6e4e62608103fefafe00",
      "individual_note": true,
      "resolvable": false,
      "notes": [
        {
          "id": 851900930,
          "type": null,
          "body": "This fixes\n\n    clang: warning: no such sysroot directory: 'macosx11.3' [-Wmissing-sysroot]\n\nby removing `-DCMAKE_OSX_SYSROOT=macosx11.3`. According to [this comment](https://discourse.cmake.org/t/specifying-cmake-osx-sysroot-breaks-xcode-projects-but-no-other-choice/2532/3), this is the correct thing to do with newer SDKs, but the Firefox team seems to be pretty adamant about [using a specific SDK version](https://firefox-source-docs.mozilla.org/widget/cocoa/sdks.html).",
          "author": {
            "id": 860017,
            "username": "geraldcombs",
            "public_email": "",
            "name": "Gerald Combs",
            "state": "active",
            "locked": false,
            "avatar_url": "https://gitlab.com/uploads/-/system/user/avatar/860017/avatar.png?v=1789887783",
            "web_url": "https://gitlab.com/geraldcombs"
          },
          "created_at": "2022-02-23T02:45:37.273Z",
          "updated_at": "2022-02-23T17:42:40.100Z",
          "system": false,
          "noteable_id": 141650986,
          "noteable_type": "MergeRequest",
          "project_id": 7898047,
          "resolvable": false,
          "confidential": false,
          "internal": false,
          "imported": false,
          "imported_from": "none",
          "noteable_iid": 6279,
          "commands_changes": {}
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "564fe0a6f3e8cb238274bb844a67a22e69336571",
      "individual_note": true,
      "resolvable": false,
      "notes": [
        {
          "id": 852056522,
          "type": null,
          "body": "*Definitely* set the minimum deployment target appropriately, as the Firefox article says; that prevents the development tools from wiring in a dependency on a newer OS.  (That was an issue a while ago - there was a new feature added to Mach-O in, as I remember, Snow Leopard, so that, if that feature was used in the executable, it wouldn't run on Leopard, whose run-time linker didn't support it.  We were still supporting Leopard at that time, so we had to make sure the minimum deployment target was set to Leopard, even when building on Snow Leopard or later.)\n\nBuilding with an SDK for an version later than the minimum deployment target means that your code could end up using APIs not available in the minimum deployment target and any other versions between that and the SDK version.  The configuration script will find them and define the appropriate #defines, and any tests of #defines in SDK headers will also find #defines in the SDK version.\n\nThis means that code needs to be careful about using APIs, #define/enum values, structure/union members, etc. that were introduced after the minimum deployment target.\n\nThe Firefox article speaks mainly of AppKit APIs; we don't directly use much if anything of AppKit - that's largely handled by Qt, and I don't know what SDK *it* was built with, but the way you deal with SDK vs. OS on which you're running APIs, for *most* libraries, is to rely on Darwin's [\"weak linking\"](https://developer.apple.com/library/archive/documentation/MacOSX/Conceptual/BPFrameworks/Concepts/WeakLinking.html) mechanism, wherein a reference to a symbol that's marked as \"weak\" will not cause an error if you run on an OS where it's not present; instead, the address of the symbol evaluates to NULL, and the compiler knows better than to optimize away tests of whether the symbol is NULL, so you just check whether it's NULL and don't call it (or otherwise refer to it) if it is.  (The Apple article speaks of frameworks, but it applies to Ordinary Boring Shared Libraries as well.)\n\nSadly, however, while Apple not only uses weak linking for their higher-level frameworks, but also uses it for the \"core OS\" UNIX APIs, they don't use it for at least one library they get from a third party (even though they've made some significant changes)...\n\n...libpcap.  That's why `request_high_resolution_timestamp()` does some extra stuff on macOS, using the old \"`dlopen()` the library and `dlsym()` the routine that might or might not be there\" trick (which I think we used to do with libpcap on Linux for some reason I no longer remember).  They *did*, however, do it for zlib.\n\nSome Wireshark developer found that a bit annoying and, also being a libpcap developer, put into libpcap 1.10 a scheme to allow Apple to fairly straightforwardly set up libpcap to do weak linking.  Unfortunately, Monterey still uses 1.9, so that's not available yet; hopefully Apple will pick that up.  It *might* be the case that building against an SDK that does the appropriate weak linking stuff will result in code that will work even with versions of libpcap that  didn't do the weak linking stuff, i.e. weak importing is sufficient, weak exporting isn't necessary.\n\nSo using the default SDK *should* be sufficient, as long as we're careful about using APIs that aren't in all macOS versions dating back to the minimum deployment target - and doing that would allow us to use those APIs when available on the platform and avoid them when they're not available, with a single binary build for all OS versions.",
          "author": {
            "id": 5636682,
            "username": "guyharris",
            "public_email": "",
            "name": "Guy Harris",
            "state": "active",
            "locked": false,
            "avatar_url": "https://secure.gravatar.com/avatar/1b7c243a9b1d6587a9a9067e5d83aba1e87212df7c4eeae3690f791ecbe4675a?s=80&d=identicon",
            "web_url": "https://gitlab.com/guyharris"
          },
          "created_at": "2022-02-23T07:56:55.027Z",
          "updated_at": "2022-02-23T07:56:55.027Z",
